WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.290 --> 00:00:02.320
- [Instructor] We're told
that Katie made a table

00:00:02.320 --> 00:00:06.020
to show how much time she
spent on homework last week,

00:00:06.020 --> 00:00:08.010
and so we can see the different subjects

00:00:08.010 --> 00:00:10.930
and then how much she
spent in terms of hours.

00:00:10.930 --> 00:00:12.860
So in math, she spent 3/4 of an hour,

00:00:12.860 --> 00:00:14.790
reading, 7/8 of an hour,

00:00:14.790 --> 00:00:17.070
writing, 3/6 of an hour,

00:00:17.070 --> 00:00:19.400
and then science, 5/10 of an hour.

00:00:19.400 --> 00:00:21.430
And then they ask us on which activities

00:00:21.430 --> 00:00:26.010
did Katie spend more than 2/3 of an hour?

00:00:26.010 --> 00:00:28.903
So pause this video and see
if you can figure that out.

00:00:30.050 --> 00:00:32.680
All right, so we essentially
have to figure out

00:00:32.680 --> 00:00:37.620
which of these fractions are
greater than 2/3 of an hour?

00:00:37.620 --> 00:00:39.020
Which are greater than 2/3?

00:00:39.020 --> 00:00:41.530
And this is all in terms of hours.

00:00:41.530 --> 00:00:44.580
So first let's just think
about representing 2/3,

00:00:44.580 --> 00:00:46.130
so let me do it like this,

00:00:46.130 --> 00:00:47.190
and I'm going to hand draw it

00:00:47.190 --> 00:00:49.130
so it's not going to be perfect.

00:00:49.130 --> 00:00:52.770
But if this is a whole right over here,

00:00:52.770 --> 00:00:55.150
I could split it into
three equal sections,

00:00:55.150 --> 00:00:56.630
so I'm gonna try to do that.

00:00:56.630 --> 00:00:59.220
Let me see, does that look about right?

00:00:59.220 --> 00:01:00.440
So three equal sections,

00:01:00.440 --> 00:01:03.540
so that would be 1/3 that would be 1/3,

00:01:03.540 --> 00:01:04.870
and then that would be 1/3,

00:01:04.870 --> 00:01:08.380
and then 2/3 would be two of them,

00:01:08.380 --> 00:01:09.640
so I'll pick these first two.

00:01:09.640 --> 00:01:11.140
Well, it doesn't have
to be those first two,

00:01:11.140 --> 00:01:14.003
so that's 1/3 and then 2/3.

00:01:15.070 --> 00:01:17.150
So let's see if we can
draw a similar visual

00:01:17.150 --> 00:01:18.860
for each of these.

00:01:18.860 --> 00:01:21.660
So what about 3/4?

00:01:21.660 --> 00:01:22.720
So once again,

00:01:22.720 --> 00:01:25.930
let's make this a whole.

00:01:25.930 --> 00:01:27.720
And now if I want to
think in terms of fourths,

00:01:27.720 --> 00:01:29.580
I have to divide it into
four equal sections,

00:01:29.580 --> 00:01:30.776
so let's see.

00:01:30.776 --> 00:01:32.970
If I divide it, that will
be two equal sections,

00:01:32.970 --> 00:01:34.590
and then I can divide each of those

00:01:34.590 --> 00:01:37.530
into two equal sections, so let's see.

00:01:37.530 --> 00:01:39.320
Maybe something like this,

00:01:39.320 --> 00:01:42.050
and then like this.

00:01:42.050 --> 00:01:45.050
So it's hand drawn, but these
are four equal sections,

00:01:45.050 --> 00:01:46.870
and so this would be a fourth,

00:01:46.870 --> 00:01:49.180
that would be a fourth,
and that would be a fourth,

00:01:49.180 --> 00:01:50.013
and that would be a fourth.

00:01:50.013 --> 00:01:51.560
4/4 make a whole,

00:01:51.560 --> 00:01:54.880
so three of those fourths would be one,

00:01:54.880 --> 00:01:57.220
two, and then three.

00:01:57.220 --> 00:01:59.820
And so you can see, and
I've drawn it pretty close.

00:01:59.820 --> 00:02:02.040
It's not perfect 'cause it's hand drawn,

00:02:02.040 --> 00:02:06.930
but you can see that 3/4 is
more of a whole than 2/3.

00:02:06.930 --> 00:02:08.800
It's greater than 2/3.

00:02:08.800 --> 00:02:12.450
So Katie spent more than
2/3 of an hour on math

00:02:12.450 --> 00:02:14.810
because 3/4 is greater than 2/3,

00:02:14.810 --> 00:02:16.870
so I like this one right over there.

00:02:16.870 --> 00:02:18.840
I'll just put a square
or circle around the ones

00:02:18.840 --> 00:02:20.110
that she spent that extra time on

00:02:20.110 --> 00:02:23.430
or the more, the greater
than 2/3 of an hour on.

00:02:23.430 --> 00:02:26.008
And I'll think about reading, 7/8.

00:02:26.008 --> 00:02:28.570
So to compare that, I will, once again,

00:02:28.570 --> 00:02:29.963
make a whole here,

00:02:30.860 --> 00:02:33.050
and then I want to think about 7/8,

00:02:33.050 --> 00:02:35.300
so I'm gonna split into
eight equal sections.

00:02:35.300 --> 00:02:38.050
So let's see, that will split
into two equal sections,

00:02:38.050 --> 00:02:42.730
and then, then I can go
to four equal sections,

00:02:42.730 --> 00:02:45.690
and then if I split
each of those into two,

00:02:45.690 --> 00:02:48.080
this would be eight equal sections,

00:02:48.080 --> 00:02:52.960
so it may look something like this.

00:02:52.960 --> 00:02:56.750
Not perfect, but I think
it will get the job done.

00:02:56.750 --> 00:02:58.620
So each of these are 1/8,

00:02:58.620 --> 00:03:03.620
so 7/8 are going to be one, two, three,

00:03:03.850 --> 00:03:08.850
four, five, six, and 7/8.

00:03:08.860 --> 00:03:10.060
Clearly, once again,

00:03:10.060 --> 00:03:12.940
greater than our 2/3
that we have in purple.

00:03:12.940 --> 00:03:17.250
So she spent more than
2/3 of an hour on reading.

00:03:17.250 --> 00:03:18.310
Now what about writing?

00:03:18.310 --> 00:03:22.160
3/6, what would that look like?

00:03:22.160 --> 00:03:25.470
Well, I'll do another one right over here.

00:03:25.470 --> 00:03:27.970
So if that is a whole,

00:03:27.970 --> 00:03:29.480
well, actually let me
split it to thirds first,

00:03:29.480 --> 00:03:31.300
since we already have a
bit of a reference there.

00:03:31.300 --> 00:03:33.640
We can look up there and we can see.

00:03:33.640 --> 00:03:35.970
Those are thirds and if we
split each of those into two,

00:03:35.970 --> 00:03:37.440
we're going to have sixths,

00:03:37.440 --> 00:03:40.200
'cause we'll have six equal sections.

00:03:40.200 --> 00:03:43.733
So it would look something like that,

00:03:44.670 --> 00:03:47.000
and then three of those six,

00:03:47.000 --> 00:03:51.040
well, that's one, two, and three.

00:03:51.040 --> 00:03:54.160
So we can see that 3/6 is less than 2/3

00:03:54.160 --> 00:03:55.910
so I'm not gonna circle that one.

00:03:55.910 --> 00:03:58.590
She didn't spend more than
2/3 of an hour on writing,

00:03:58.590 --> 00:04:01.970
and then last, but not least, science.

00:04:01.970 --> 00:04:03.660
5/10.

00:04:03.660 --> 00:04:04.910
Well once again,

00:04:04.910 --> 00:04:08.240
we could make a whole here,

00:04:08.240 --> 00:04:09.230
and let's see.

00:04:09.230 --> 00:04:11.250
I want to do into 10 equal sections,

00:04:11.250 --> 00:04:13.760
so that's two equal sections,

00:04:13.760 --> 00:04:16.149
and each of these, I can do
into five equal sections.

00:04:16.149 --> 00:04:16.982
Let's see.

00:04:16.982 --> 00:04:20.940
One, two, three,

00:04:20.940 --> 00:04:24.570
four, five, I can do a
little bit neater than that,

00:04:24.570 --> 00:04:27.540
three, four and five,

00:04:27.540 --> 00:04:29.853
and then one,

00:04:31.010 --> 00:04:33.403
two, three,

00:04:34.440 --> 00:04:36.820
four and five.

00:04:36.820 --> 00:04:38.120
I can make it a little bit neater,

00:04:38.120 --> 00:04:39.210
but I think this will get us

00:04:39.210 --> 00:04:40.840
to where we need to get to.

00:04:40.840 --> 00:04:42.020
So these are each 1/10.

00:04:42.020 --> 00:04:44.190
I could write 1/10, 1/10,

00:04:44.190 --> 00:04:45.570
I could do that for all 10 of these,

00:04:45.570 --> 00:04:47.620
but we care about five of these tenths,

00:04:47.620 --> 00:04:50.120
so one, two,

00:04:50.120 --> 00:04:54.180
three, four, five of those tenths.

00:04:54.180 --> 00:04:57.280
And notice, 5/10 is the
exact same thing as 3/6,

00:04:57.280 --> 00:05:00.450
and like 3/6, it is less than 2/3.

00:05:00.450 --> 00:05:02.820
So which activities did
Katie spend more than 2/3

00:05:02.820 --> 00:05:04.482
of an hour on?

00:05:04.482 --> 00:05:05.782
Clearly, math and reading.

